  '''
# ---
# name: test_help_messages[bundle]
  '''
                                                                                  
   Usage: root bundle [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...                                 
                                                                                  
   Manages Snowflake Code Bundles.                                                
                                                                                  
  +- Options --------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | --help  -h        Show this message and exit.                                |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Commands -------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | alter     Alters a code bundle by renaming it or adding a new version.       |
  | cancel    Cancels an async code bundle execution.                            |
  | create    Creates a code bundle.                                             |
  | delete    Drops a code bundle.                                               |
  | execute   Executes a code bundle at the given entrypoint.                    |
  | history   Returns the execution history of code bundles.                     |
  | list      Lists code bundles.                                                |
  | status    Returns the execution status of an async code bundle execution.    |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help_messages[connection.add]
  '''
                                                                                  
   Usage: root connection add [OPTIONS] [global options]                          
                                                                                  
   Adds a connection to configuration file.                                       
                                                                                  
  +- Options --------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | --connection-name              -n      TEXT     Name of the new connection.  |
  | --account,--accountname        -a      TEXT     Account name to use when     |
  |                                                 authenticating with          |
  |                                                 Snowflake.                   |
  | --user,--username              -u      TEXT     Username to connect to       |
  |                                                 Snowflake.                   |
  | --password                     -p      TEXT     Snowflake password.          |
  | --role,--rolename              -r      TEXT     Role to use on Snowflake.    |
  | --warehouse                    -w      TEXT     Warehouse to use on          |
  |                                                 Snowflake.                   |
  | --database,--dbname            -d      TEXT     Database to use on           |
  |                                                 Snowflake.                   |
  | --schema,--schemaname          -s      TEXT     Schema to use on Snowflake.  |
  | --host                         -h      TEXT     Host name the connection     |
  |                                                 attempts to connect to       |
  |                                                 Snowflake.                   |
  | --port                         -P      INTEGER  Port to communicate with on  |
  |                                                 the host.                    |
  | --protocol                             TEXT     Protocol to use for the      |
  |                                                 connection, for example      |
  |                                                 https.                       |
  | --region                       -R      TEXT     Region name if not the       |
  |                                                 default Snowflake            |
  |                                                 deployment.                  |
  | --authenticator                -A      TEXT     Chosen authenticator, if     |
  |                                                 other than password-based    |
  | --workload-identity-provider   -W      TEXT     Workload identity provider   |
  |                                                 type                         |
  | --private-key,--private-key-…  -k      TEXT     Path to file containing      |
  |                                                 private key                  |
  | --token-file-path              -t      TEXT     Path to file with an OAuth   |
  |                                                 token that should be used    |
  |                                                 when connecting to Snowflake |
  | --secondary-roles                      TEXT     Secondary roles mode applied |
  |                                                 when the session starts.     |
  |                                                 Supported values are ALL and |
  |                                                 NONE; pass NONE to run the   |
  |                                                 session only with the        |
  |                                                 primary role.                |
  | --server-session-keep-alive                     Enable server-side session   |
  |                                                 keep-alive to prevent the    |
  |                                                 session from timing out      |
  |                                                 during long operations.      |
  | --client-store-temporary-cre…                   Store the temporary          |
  |                                                 credential.                  |
  | --default                                       If provided the connection   |
  |                                                 will be configured as        |
  |                                                 default connection.          |
  | --no-interactive                                Disable prompting.           |
  | --help                                          Show this message and exit.  |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Global options -------------------------------------------------------------+
  | Global options: --format, --verbose, --debug, --silent,                      |
  | --enhanced-exit-codes, --decimal-precision                                   |
  |                                                                              |
  | Run `snow --help` for full descriptions, or use `--help-all` on this         |
  | command.                                                                     |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Usage notes ----------------------------------------------------------------+
  | The snow connection add command adds the connection to your default          |
  | config.toml file. For more information, see                                  |
  | /developer-guide/snowflake-cli/connecting/connect.                           |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Examples -------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | To add a connection, run the following:                                      |
  | snow connection add                                                          |
  | Enter connection name: <connection_name>                                     |
  | Enter account: <account>                                                     |
  | Enter user: <user-name>                                                      |
  | Enter password: <password>                                                   |
  | Enter role: <role-name>                                                      |
  | Enter warehouse: <warehouse-name>                                            |
  | Enter database: <database-name>                                              |
  | Enter schema: <schema-name>                                                  |
  | Enter host: <host-name>                                                      |
  | Enter port: <port-number>                                                    |
  | Enter region: <region-name>                                                  |
  | Enter authenticator: <authentication-method>                                 |
  | Enter private key file: <path-to-private-key-file>                           |
  | Enter token file path: <path-to-mfa-token>                                   |
  | Do you want to configure key pair authentication? [y/N]: y                   |
  | Key length [2048]: <key-length>                                              |
  | Output path [~/.ssh]: <path-to-output-file>                                  |
  | Private key passphrase: <key-description>                                    |
  | Wrote new connection <connection-name> to config.toml                        |
  | Output: Wrote new connection my_conn to <user-home>/.snowflake/config.toml   |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Related topics -------------------------------------------------------------+
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/index          |
  | • Snowflake CLI command reference                                            |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/overview                                                 |
  | • Connection command reference                                               |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/overview                             |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/list-connections                     |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/test-connection                      |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help_messages[connection.generate-jwt]
  '''
                                                                                  
   Usage: root connection generate-jwt [OPTIONS] [global options] [connection     
   options]                                                                       
                                                                                  
   Generate a JWT token, which will be printed out and displayed..                
                                                                                  
  +- Options --------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | --help  -h        Show this message and exit.                                |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Global and connection options ----------------------------------------------+
  | Connection options: --connection, --host, --port, --protocol, --account,     |
  | --user, --password, --authenticator, --workload-identity-provider,           |
  | --private-key-file, --token, --token-file-path, --database, --schema,        |
  | --role, --warehouse, --temporary-connection, --mfa-passcode, --enable-diag,  |
  | --diag-log-path, --diag-allowlist-path, --oauth-client-id,                   |
  | --oauth-client-secret, --oauth-authorization-url, --oauth-token-request-url, |
  | --oauth-redirect-uri, --oauth-scope, --oauth-disable-pkce,                   |
  | --oauth-enable-refresh-tokens, --oauth-enable-single-use-refresh-tokens,     |
  | --client-store-temporary-credential, --secondary-roles,                      |
  | --server-session-keep-alive                                                  |
  | Global options: --format, --verbose, --debug, --silent,                      |
  | --enhanced-exit-codes, --decimal-precision                                   |
  |                                                                              |
  | Run `snow --help` for full descriptions, or use `--help-all` on this         |
  | command.                                                                     |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Usage notes ----------------------------------------------------------------+
  | The snow connection generate-jwt command generates a JWT (JSON Web Token)    |
  | that you can use for key-pair authentication when connecting to Snowflake.   |
  | You can use the token to connect to Snowflake from any Snowflake             |
  | application, such as the SQL REST API or the Snowflake REST APIs.            |
  |                                                                              |
  | The command prompts you for a private key passphrase to complete the         |
  | connection. You can avoid the prompt by providing the passphrase in the      |
  | PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE environment variable.                                 |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Examples -------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | This example generates a token for account TEST and user JDOE, using the     |
  | private key from rsa_key.p8:                                                 |
  | snow connection generate-jwt --user JDOE --account TEST                      |
  | --private-key-file=rsa_key.p8                                                |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Related topics -------------------------------------------------------------+
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/index          |
  | • Snowflake CLI command reference                                            |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/overview                                                 |
  | • Connection command reference                                               |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/overview                             |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/add-connection                       |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/list-connections                     |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/set-default-connection               |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_help_messages[connection.generate-workload-identity-token]
  '''
                                                                                  
   Usage: root connection generate-workload-identity-token [OPTIONS] [global      
   options] [connection options]                                                  
                                                                                  
   Generate a workload identity token for authenticating to Snowflake via a       
   Workload Identity Federation (WIF) provider (AWS, AZURE, GCP, or OIDC). The    
   token is printed to stdout.                                                    
                                                                                  
  +- Options --------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | --help  -h        Show this message and exit.                                |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Global and connection options ----------------------------------------------+
  | Connection options: --connection, --host, --port, --protocol, --account,     |
  | --user, --password, --authenticator, --workload-identity-provider,           |
  | --private-key-file, --token, --token-file-path, --database, --schema,        |
  | --role, --warehouse, --temporary-connection, --mfa-passcode, --enable-diag,  |
  | --diag-log-path, --diag-allowlist-path, --oauth-client-id,                   |
  | --oauth-client-secret, --oauth-authorization-url, --oauth-token-request-url, |
  | --oauth-redirect-uri, --oauth-scope, --oauth-disable-pkce,                   |
  | --oauth-enable-refresh-tokens, --oauth-enable-single-use-refresh-tokens,     |
  | --client-store-temporary-credential, --secondary-roles,                      |
  | --server-session-keep-alive                                                  |
  | Global options: --format, --verbose, --debug, --silent,                      |
  | --enhanced-exit-codes, --decimal-precision                                   |
  |                                                                              |
  | Run `snow --help` for full descriptions, or use `--help-all` on this         |
  | command.                                                                     |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Usage notes ----------------------------------------------------------------+
  | The snow connection generate-workload-identity-token command generates a     |
  | workload identity token for the current execution environment. Use this      |
  | token for workload identity authentication when connecting to Snowflake from |
  | a workload running in AWS, GCP, Azure, or any OIDC-compliant environment.    |
  |                                                                              |
  | You can select the provider with the --workload-identity-provider option, or |
  | let Snowflake CLI resolve it from the active connection configuration.       |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Examples -------------------------------------------------------------------+
  | Generate a workload identity token using the provider configured in the      |
  | active connection:                                                           |
  | snow connection generate-workload-identity-token                             |
  |                                                                              |
  | Generate a token for a specific provider, such as AWS:                       |
  | snow connection generate-workload-identity-token                             |
  | --workload-identity-provider AWS                                             |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  +- Related topics -------------------------------------------------------------+
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/index          |
  | • Snowflake CLI command reference                                            |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/overview                                                 |
  | • Connection command reference                                               |
  |   https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/overview                             |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/add-connection                       |
  | • https://docs.snowflake.com/en/developer-guide/snowflake-cli/               |
  |   command-reference/connection-commands/generate-jwt                         |
  +------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
  
  
  '''
